; `use` inside a proof-like block would make the result look proven although it relies on an
; unproven axiom: here `assume A` + `use B` would give `A implies B` "by impl-intro".  So `use`
; is only allowed at the top level of a file, or inside a `sandbox` or `expect` (whose content
; is discarded anyway).  This can't use `expect` itself, since inside `expect` `use` is allowed.
bool A, B
sandbox
    assume A
        use B        ; fine: the sandbox is discarded
assume A
    use B
;;; EvalError: `use` is not allowed inside `assume`
